I’ll inspect the diffraction pattern and metadata first, then identify candidate phases and build a quantitative GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the submitted CIFs, profile, statistics, report, and `final.gpx` internally consistent and flag any ambiguity that the data cannot resolve.

The chemistry points toward manganese tungstates, but I’m treating precursor carryover and competing Mn–W–O polymorphs as live possibilities. The scan is high-resolution enough to use peak positions and systematic absences first, then let whole-pattern refinement decide whether minor crystalline phases are warranted.

Peak positions and all strong/medium reflections are consistent with monoclinic wolframite-type MnWO₄; the supposed alternatives Mn₃WO₆, Mn₃O₄, and WO₃ predict strong lines that are absent. I’m now refining the MnWO₄ cell, specimen displacement, line profile, and intensities, then I’ll inspect the residual for any defensible minor phase rather than adding one merely to lower the fit statistic.

The whole-pattern residual is now low and, importantly, does not contain a second set of Bragg positions. Remaining structure in the difference curve is concentrated around MnWO₄ peaks, which points to line-shape/instrument effects rather than another phase. I’m tightening that profile model and checking that any extra correction stays physically meaningful; refinements that drive displacement parameters or thermal factors to nonphysical values will be rejected.

Phase screening confirms the submitted crystalline model should remain single-phase: Mn₃O₄ refined to only 0.16(18) wt%, and several WO₃ polymorph tests produced small, model-dependent apparent amounts driven mainly by overlap with MnWO₄ peak-shape residuals rather than independent diagnostic lines. I’ll report that as a trace-level uncertainty, not as a reliably quantified second phase.

The final one-phase refinement is converged. I’m writing the machine-readable result and concise uncertainty report now, then I’ll reload every submitted artifact to verify that the CIF, profile, statistics, and project are mutually consistent.

The deliverables themselves are in place. One validation process lacked the crystal-structure parser available in the other scientific environment, so I’m splitting the checks between the GSAS-II and crystallography environments; this does not affect the refinement or files.

All cross-checks now pass: the 8,301 profile rows are numerically identical to the arrays stored in `final.gpx`, the reported residuals match the project, and the CIF parses as P2/c MnWO4 with chemically sensible Mn–O and W–O coordination. I’m removing only the refinement trials I generated, leaving a clean submission set.

Completed the PXRD phase analysis.

The only reliably supported crystalline phase is monoclinic wolframite MnWO4, space group P2/c, at 100.0 wt% normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Final fit: Rwp 5.068%, Rp 3.813%, GOF 1.467.

Trace WO3 below roughly 1 wt% cannot be excluded reliably; Mn3O4 and Mn3WO6 were not supported. Amorphous content cannot be determined without an internal standard.

